{"text": "EDITORIAL\n\nHealth Risks of Passive Smoking\n\n\u2018This issue of Environment International contains & con-\ntribution on the carcinogenic risk of nonsmokers who\nare exposed to tobacco smoke. This contribution is co-\nauthored by Repace and Lowry, who have been active in\nthe study of pollution of indoor air primarily caused by\nsmoking.\nDue to the controversial nature of the paper by\nRepace and Lowry and its significance, added measures\nwere required to assure adequate peer review and the\ndocumentation of the underlying information, includ-\ning assumptions.\nDespite the efforts by the authors, the reviewers, and\nthe editors, there is no doubt that this paper willbe dis-\n\u2018cussed and its conclusions willbe disputed by various in-\nlerested partes.\nThere is no question that tobacco smoke may\u2019 con-\ntribute significantly to the pollution of the indoor air by\nincreasing the concentration of numerous pollutants,\nsuch as CO, polycyclic aromatic compounds, and a\nlarge number of other toxic materials. A number of\n\u2018chemical compounds found in tobacco smoke are human,\n\u2018carcinogens. Once these materials are in the indoor air,\nthey may be inhaled.\nIn their paper, Repace and Lowry compute the car-\nsinogenic risk of (active) smoking per mg tar inhaled,\nsalculate the concentration of particles inthe indoor:\nand combine the two to estimate the carcinogenic risk of\n\no1en.an20 $553.0 \u00ab 99\nCCopynighe 1985 Peteameon Pres Lid.\n\n4 passive smoker. Subsequently, they compare their re-\nsults with those of several epidemiological studies. In the\nnext step, the authors attempt to show agreement be\n\u2018tween the two approaches and succeed only moderately\n\u2018There may be s\u00e9veral important reasons for the ap-\nparent contradiction found by Repace and Lowry. For\nexample, the dose-response curve may not be linear in\nthe region covered by active and passive smokers. A\nmore important reason may be the difference between\nthe inhalation mode of these to groups. An active\nsmoker bypasses the nasal passage and thus robs the\nlung of an important filtration device. At least 8070 of\nthe population breathes through the nose; therefore a\npassive smoker retains a significant fraction of particles\nin the nasal passages, thus making it unavailable to the\nlung. Obviously, per mg available, zhe lung of a passive\nsmoker is exposed to significantly lower levels of par-\nticles than an active smoker,\nDespite these and other complicating factors, Repace\nand Lowry make a convincing case for public health\nhazards of public smoking. The unquestionable fasts\nare that tobacco smoke exposes nonsmokers to some\npotent carcinogens. Accordingly. the relevant question\nisnot whether passive smoking poses a carcinogenic rish\nbbut what the magnitude of the carcinogenic risk is. Con:\nsidering the size of the population of passive smokers,\nthe risk could be significant,\n\nA. Alan Moghissi\n\n71132-0046", "words": [{"text": "EDITORIAL", "x0": 45, "y0": 205, "x1": 131, "y1": 218, "conf": 96.0}, {"text": "Health", "x0": 44, "y0": 286, "x1": 79, "y1": 295, "conf": 81.0}, {"text": "Risks", "x0": 84, "y0": 285, "x1": 113, "y1": 295, "conf": 93.0}, {"text": "of", "x0": 118, "y0": 286, "x1": 128, "y1": 295, "conf": 96.0}, {"text": "Passive", "x0": 133, "y0": 286, "x1": 175, "y1": 295, "conf": 96.0}, {"text": "Smoking", "x0": 180, "y0": 286, "x1": 227, "y1": 297, "conf": 94.0}, {"text": "\u2018This", "x0": 44, "y0": 314, "x1": 64, "y1": 323, "conf": 44.0}, {"text": "issue", "x0": 68, "y0": 316, "x1": 91, "y1": 323, "conf": 44.0}, {"text": "of", "x0": 94, "y0": 314, "x1": 105, "y1": 323, "conf": 96.0}, {"text": "Environment", "x0": 108, "y0": 314, "x1": 173, "y1": 323, "conf": 78.0}, {"text": "International", "x0": 176, "y0": 315, "x1": 241, "y1": 324, "conf": 89.0}, {"text": "contains", "x0": 244, "y0": 317, "x1": 285, "y1": 324, "conf": 91.0}, {"text": "&", "x0": 288, "y0": 318, "x1": 293, "y1": 324, "conf"